# Example PAM stack entry for pam_ssoossh, packaged as documentation only --
# ssoossh-pam does not write to /etc/pam.d on install. Copy the
# `auth` line below into /etc/pam.d/sudo (and /etc/pam.d/su, if wanted)
# above the existing pam_unix.so line, per the runbook in
# docs/deployment.md.
#
# `sufficient` means a successful browser approval satisfies the auth
# stack on its own; pam_unix.so still runs and can satisfy it independently
# (e.g. a local emergency password) unless removed.
#
# Module path is installed by the package:
#   deb (x86_64): /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/security/pam_ssoossh.so
#   deb (aarch64): /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/security/pam_ssoossh.so
#   rpm (any arch): /usr/lib64/security/pam_ssoossh.so
#
# Only the `auth` management group is implemented -- do not add pam_ssoossh.so
# to account/password/session lines.
#
# Arguments are `key=value`, or a bare `key` for a boolean flag. Per
# pam.conf(5), a value containing spaces must be bracketed --
# key=[a value with spaces] -- libpam strips the brackets before this module
# ever sees them, so no special handling is needed here.
#
#   server=URL              (required) base URL of the ssoosshd server, e.g.
#                            https://ssoosshd.example.com. Missing or empty
#                            fails every login with PAM_USER_UNKNOWN.
#
#   trusted-ca-file=PATH    (required) authorized_keys-format file listing
#                            the CA(s) trusted to sign PAM certificates, one
#                            per line. Rotate CAs by editing this file --
#                            no restart needed. Missing or empty fails every
#                            login with PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA.
#
#   principals-map=PATH     (optional) YAML file mapping a local account
#                            name to the certificate principals allowed to
#                            assume it, e.g.:
#                              alice:
#                                - alice
#                                - admin
#                            Omitted, or a path that fails to load (missing
#                            file, malformed YAML): the certificate must
#                            instead carry the exact local account name as
#                            one of its principals.
#
#   skew-tolerance=DURATION (default 2s) clock-skew allowance applied
#                            symmetrically to both ends of the certificate's
#                            validity window, e.g. skew-tolerance=5s. Go
#                            duration syntax
#                            (https://pkg.go.dev/time#ParseDuration); an
#                            unparseable value silently falls back to the
#                            default. Choose this together with the server's
#                            cert_options.pam.valid_duration.
#
#   timeout=DURATION        (default 60s) how long a login attempt waits for
#                            browser approval before giving up, e.g.
#                            timeout=2m. Same duration syntax and fallback
#                            behavior as skew-tolerance.
#
#   insecure-skip-verify     (default false) skip TLS certificate
#                            verification when talking to the server. Bare
#                            form or =true enables it; do not use outside
#                            testing.
#
#   debug                    (default off, i.e. "false"/absent) log at debug
#                            level. Bare form, or any value other than
#                            "false"/"stdout", logs through syslog (facility
#                            authpriv, tag "ssoossh"); debug=stdout writes
#                            debug lines to stdout instead of syslog, mainly
#                            useful with the pamtest harness (see
#                            pam_ssoossh/testing/), since on a real login
#                            stdout belongs to whatever invoked PAM. Every
#                            invocation logs its version at info level
#                            through syslog regardless of this setting.
#
# auth  sufficient  pam_ssoossh.so  server=https://ssoosshd.example.com  trusted-ca-file=/etc/ssoossh/ca.pub
#
# With the optional settings also set:
# auth  sufficient  pam_ssoossh.so  server=https://ssoosshd.example.com  trusted-ca-file=/etc/ssoossh/ca.pub  principals-map=/etc/ssoossh/principals.yaml  skew-tolerance=5s  timeout=90s
